# shorts-scraper — configuration. Copy to .env.local and fill in.
#
# NOTHING IN THIS FILE EVER CARRIES A VALUE. Names only.
# `.gitignore` keeps `.env.local` out of the tree; this file exists so nobody
# has to guess what `.env.local` is supposed to contain.
#
# THE DEFAULT NEEDS NONE OF THIS. With no `.env.local` at all the app runs: two
# of the five platforms read through yt-dlp with no key, no quota and nobody
# billed, and the other three say on the page exactly why they cannot run. A
# missing variable here never turns into a silent empty list — that is the one
# rule this whole repo turns on.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The quota harness's adapter switch
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `ytdlp` (default) — credential-free. Reads public pages. No key, no quota, no
#   billing.
# `api` — the YouTube Data API v3 path. Needs an operator credential saved on
#   /admin/credentials.
#
# NARROWED, 2026-09-02 (plan amendment A2). This used to choose the ONE source
# adapter for the whole tool, back when the tool was YouTube-shaped. It no
# longer does: there are five platforms now, each with its own adapter, and
# lib/platform/registry.ts is the only thing that decides which one runs.
# The single remaining reader is verify/quota.ts, where this variable decides
# whether the quota check is inert (`ytdlp`, nothing spends units) or required
# (`api`). Left in place rather than deleted because the harness still reads it
# and a variable that vanishes from this file is a variable nobody can find.
SOURCE_ADAPTER=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The two thresholds that define the product
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both have defaults, and both defaults cite a person and a date. That is the
# rule in lib/config.ts: A NUMBER MAY HAVE A DEFAULT WHEN SOMEBODY WITH THE
# AUTHORITY TO SET IT SAID IT OUT LOUD, AND NOT WHEN IT WAS READ OFF SOMEBODY
# ELSE'S DOCUMENTATION.

# Optional. Defaults to 120 — the client's own words, 2026-09-01: "2 minutes
# max is length". Config rather than a literal because YouTube's own Shorts
# ceiling has moved before. Must agree with shorts_scraper.short_max_seconds()
# in supabase/migrations/; tests/config-agreement.test.ts fails if the two
# drift apart.
#
# IT CANNOT BE APPLIED TO INSTAGRAM'S OFFICIAL API AT ALL. Verified 2026-09-04
# against Meta's own field list for an Instagram media object: there is no
# duration field of any kind on it. So a row that came from the Instagram
# official path has been filtered on views only, and the UI has to say so.
# Plan amendment A4 records this; it is a product hole, not a TODO.
SHORT_MAX_SECONDS=

# Optional. Defaults to 500000 — Erik, 2026-09-02, describing the whole tool in
# one sentence: "come back with shorts over 500k views categorized by platform,
# AXE the rest."
#
# DELIBERATELY ONE NUMBER FOR ALL FIVE PLATFORMS. A million views on YouTube is
# not a million on X, but nobody has told us what those numbers are, and
# inventing four of them would put a business judgement in a config file under
# cover of a default. When Erik gives per-platform figures they belong in
# lib/config.ts, cited the same way.
#
# `MIN_VIEWS=0` IS REJECTED rather than read as "no threshold". It is a request
# to turn the product's one promise off, and far more likely to be a misread
# env file than an intention. Deleting the filter is a code change and it
# should look like one.
MIN_VIEWS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Platform seeds — WHICH ACCOUNTS EACH PLATFORM READS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Comma-, whitespace- or newline-separated. The key is DERIVED from the
# platform name by lib/platform/registry.ts:seedEnvKey(), so a sixth platform
# gets its seed variable for free and there is no table here to fall out of
# step with the code.
#
# THESE ARE MEANT TO END UP AS THE FALLBACK, NOT THE PRODUCT SURFACE. Seeds an
# operator manages belong in the database, editable without a redeploy — a
# hand-edited env var is exactly what Erik asked to get away from on
# 2026-09-04, and `shorts_scraper.platform_seeds` is where they go. These
# variables are what a machine with no database has, and what a developer uses
# on a laptop. If a run's seeds came from here, that is worth knowing.
#
# WHICH SOURCE WINS, AND IT IS NOT A MERGE. lib/shorts/seeds.ts decides: with
# Supabase configured the database wins OUTRIGHT and these variables are
# ignored; with no Supabase they are the whole seed list. A union of the two
# would be wrong in the direction that costs money and lies on screen — a seed
# deactivated in the database would keep being fetched while the table showed it
# as off. Zero seeds in the database means zero, including when somebody just
# turned the last one off.
#
# So a deployment that HAS these variables and then gains a database starts with
# nothing. That does not happen quietly: the run report names exactly which of
# these the database does not hold and offers a button that copies them in.
#
# WHERE THE STORE IS ACTUALLY READ, as of 2026-09-05: the Get
# latest shorts button, and the scheduled run at /api/cron/run. `buildAdapters()`
# called with no seed store falls back to these variables, which is what
# scripts/latest.ts does on a laptop with no database.
#
# AN EMPTY SEED LIST IS NOT AN ERROR AND IS NOT AN EMPTY RESULT. The adapter
# reports "no creators have been seeded" as its unavailable reason, and the
# page renders that sentence. Nothing silently returns zero shorts.

# YouTube: channel handles (@name), channel URLs or UC… channel ids.
PLATFORM_SEEDS_YOUTUBE=

# TikTok: SEC_UIDS, NOT @handles. `MS4wLjABAAAA` followed by 64 more
# characters. Verified 2026-09-04 against two public profiles: yt-dlp cannot
# turn an @handle into the internal creator id and failed both times. Get one
# with `yt-dlp -J "https://www.tiktok.com/@someone/video/<post id>"` and take
# `channel_id`. lib/platform/tiktok.ts refuses a handle with that message
# rather than letting a run die inside a loop.
#
# TIKTOK IS THE ONE PLATFORM HERE THAT NO LONGER NEEDS A SEED TO RETURN
# ANYTHING. As of 2026-09-04 it reads through ScrapeCreators, which sells a
# trending feed by region plus keyword and hashtag search — real discovery, not
# a refresh. A seed list is still worth having: it is how you say which creators
# matter enough to check every run. What a run must never do is report the two
# as one number; "refreshed N seeded creators" and "found N new creators" are
# different sentences and only one of them was sayable about TikTok before this
# date. WHAT FORM A SEED TAKES ON THE VENDOR ROUTE IS THE ADAPTER'S TO STATE —
# read lib/platform/tiktok.ts rather than assuming the sec_uid rule above
# survived the change; it is a yt-dlp constraint, not a TikTok one.
PLATFORM_SEEDS_TIKTOK=

# Instagram: @usernames of PROFESSIONAL (Business or Creator) accounts. The
# official Business Discovery route is a lookup and not a search — it cannot
# find an account you have not named. Consumer accounts cannot be read at all.
#
# THAT RESTRICTION IS THE OFFICIAL ROUTE'S, AND SINCE 2026-09-04 THE OFFICIAL
# ROUTE IS NOT THE ONE THAT RUNS. Instagram reads through ScrapeCreators, whose
# reels endpoint takes a handle or a numeric user id and does not care whether
# the account is a professional one. Read lib/platform/instagram.ts for what the
# adapter actually accepts today; the paragraph above is kept because the
# official slot still exists and its constraint still applies to it.
PLATFORM_SEEDS_INSTAGRAM=

# X HAS NO SEED LIST AND THIS FILE USED TO CARRY ONE ANYWAY.
#
# Removed 2026-09-04. There was a `PLATFORM_SEEDS_X` line here and nothing has
# ever read it: lib/platform/registry.ts calls seedsFor() for YouTube and TikTok
# and, for X, reads a QUERY and a spend cap instead — see "X: the search, the
# cap and the window" further down. X is searched, not enumerated.
#
# It was not a harmless spare line. The splitter for a seed list breaks on
# commas AND whitespace, which is right for `@handle, @handle` and destroys
# `min_likes:20000 has:video_link -is:retweet` — so an operator who found that
# name, put a query in it and redeployed would have configured nothing, on the
# one platform where being unconfigured looks identical to being broken and the
# real fix (a bearer token, then X_SEARCH_QUERY) is somewhere else entirely.
# tests/docs.test.ts now fails the build if the name comes back.

# Facebook: Page ids. Read the warning under FACEBOOK_API_KEY first — as of
# 2026-09-04 Meta's own reference documents NO read operation on either
# /{page-id}/videos or /{page-id}/video_reels, so a seed here is currently a
# seed for an endpoint that says it cannot be read.
#
# THE VENDOR ROUTE MAKES THIS LIST LOAD-BEARING AGAIN, AND IT IS THE ONLY WAY
# FACEBOOK EVER RETURNS ANYTHING. ScrapeCreators reads a public page's reels,
# so Facebook works — but it is PAGE-SEEDED ONLY and always will be: there is no
# Facebook search endpoint on that vendor's list, and nobody else sells one
# either. An empty Facebook result is very often an empty seed list, and the
# card has to be able to say which. What form a seed takes on that route is
# lib/platform/facebook.ts's to state, not this file's.
#
# LIKELY REDUNDANT AND LEFT IN DELIBERATELY. The Facebook credential itself now
# carries a required `FACEBOOK_PAGE_ID`, because the official route can only
# read a Page the operator administers — so "which Pages" is answered by the
# credential and not by a seed list. This name exists because seed keys are
# derived from the platform name and every platform therefore has one. If the
# Facebook adapter never reads it, delete this block rather than leaving a
# variable nobody can find a reader for.
PLATFORM_SEEDS_FACEBOOK=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# X: the search, the cap and the window
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THESE THREE WERE READ BY THE CODE AND NAMED IN NO FILE UNTIL 2026-09-04, and
# that is worth a paragraph because of what it did rather than what it was.
# lib/platform/registry.ts:xConfigFor() has always read all three. An operator
# could paste a valid bearer token, press Get latest shorts, and be told X was
# unavailable — correctly, because the query was unset — with nothing in the
# repo naming the variable that would fix it. The only reachable copy of that
# name was a paragraph inside lib/platform/x.ts. .env.example's own first rule
# is that a variable nobody can find is a variable nobody can set, and X is the
# platform where being unconfigured and being broken look identical.
#
# X IS SEARCHED, NOT ENUMERATED, which is why it takes a query where YouTube and
# TikTok take a list of creators. One GET /2/tweets/search/recent returns the
# post, the duration, the view count and a playable file in a single response —
# so there is no seed list to walk, and no second hop to pay for.
#
# NONE OF THE THREE HAS A DEFAULT AND THAT IS THE DESIGN. X bills $0.005 per
# Post RETURNED, so the query IS the invoice. A default query would be this file
# choosing what somebody's card is spent on; a default cap would be this file
# choosing how much. Unset means the adapter reports itself unavailable and says
# which variable is missing, which costs nothing.

# The X API v2 recent-search query, verbatim. REQUIRED for X to run at all.
#
# A workable shape, and it is an example rather than a recommendation:
#   min_likes:20000 has:video_link -is:retweet -is:reply lang:en
#
# `min_likes:` is the only lever that narrows by popularity BEFORE the bill —
# and it is NOT the view threshold. Likes are not views; MIN_VIEWS still applies
# afterwards to what came back. Treat this as a cost control.
#
# lib/platform/x-client.ts validates the query against X's documented operator
# list before spending anything, including the three mistakes the documentation
# itself calls out (`min_faves:`, `min_retweets:`, `has:videos` — the last is
# not an operator at all; the one you want is `has:video_link`), the
# 512-character self-serve ceiling, and a query made only of operators X
# requires to be conjoined with another, which X rejects outright.
#
# WRITE IT AS ONE VALUE WITH SPACES IN IT. It is a query, not a list, and
# nothing splits it.
X_SEARCH_QUERY=

# A hard ceiling on billable Post reads for ONE run. REQUIRED; no default.
#
# THIS IS THE SPEND CAP AND IT IS THE REASON THE VARIABLE HAS NO DEFAULT. At
# $0.005 per Post returned, 100 posts is fifty cents and 20,000 posts is $100 —
# and what the number lands on is decided by whatever the query happens to match
# today, not by anything in this repo. So the ceiling is a decision somebody with
# the authority to spend has to make out loud.
#
# A VALUE THAT IS NOT A WHOLE NUMBER IS NOT TREATED AS UNSET. `two hundred`
# arrives at the adapter as NaN and produces its own message, because "nobody set
# a cap" and "somebody set a cap and typed it wrong" are different problems and
# collapsing the second into the first sends an operator to the wrong variable.
X_MAX_POSTS_PER_RUN=

# Optional. How far back one run searches, in hours.
#
# Unset means X's own recent window, which the OpenAPI spec documents as the
# last 7 days; a larger number is clamped to it either way. A SHORTER window is
# a cost control on a schedule that fires often — re-reading the same 7 days
# every hour pays for the overlap, less than you would think because X charges a
# repeated resource only once per 24 hours, but not nothing.
X_WINDOW_HOURS=

# Optional escape hatch: `tweet.fields` or `post.fields`. LEAVE IT UNSET UNTIL
# AN ERROR MESSAGE TELLS YOU TO SET IT.
#
# X's own documentation contradicts itself about what the Post fields parameter
# is called. The OpenAPI spec for GET /2/tweets/search/recent names it
# `post.fields`; every worked example on the Fields page, curl commands
# included, uses `tweet.fields`. Getting it wrong loses every metric on the
# response — which on this product means every view count, which means every row
# silently fails the threshold.
#
# The client defaults to `tweet.fields` and that is a JUDGEMENT, not a finding:
# a broken copy-pasteable example is a louder failure than a stale name in a
# generated spec. Two nets sit under it. A 400 blaming the parameter retries
# once with the other spelling for free, because a rejected request returns no
# resources and reads are billed per resource. A response that ACCEPTED the
# parameter and ignored it cannot be retried for free — that costs another page
# of Post reads — so it throws instead, naming this variable and the value to
# put in it. This line is where that instruction lands.
#
# An unrecognised value is warned about and ignored rather than thrown on: it is
# read while the status page is rendering all five platforms, and a typo in an
# optional lever must not take that page down.
X_POST_FIELDS_PARAM=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DECIDED by Erik on 2026-09-02, answering the plan's open question Q4: LookUp
# Media's own Supabase, in a database SHARED with the account's other projects,
# because a Supabase project per tool costs $10/month each and there are a lot
# of tools. The client's own account also keeps the data, the billing and the
# terms-of-service exposure on the client's side — the same principle as the
# bring-your-own-API-key design below.
#
# ONE SCHEMA PER TOOL is what makes that safe. Everything this repo creates
# lives in `shorts_scraper` and nothing lives in `public`; the clients in
# lib/supabase/ are pinned to that schema and tests/migrations.test.ts fails the
# build if a migration reaches into `public`. See lib/supabase/config.ts.
#
# TWO OPERATOR STEPS THAT ARE EASY TO MISS:
#
#   1. Settings -> API -> Exposed schemas: ADD `shorts_scraper` to the existing
#      list. Do not replace it — the other entries are co-tenant projects, and
#      removing one takes that app down. Without this every query returns
#      PGRST106 and it looks like a broken build.
#
#   2. Never run `supabase config push` or `supabase db reset` against this
#      project. Both are project-wide, not schema-scoped. supabase/config.toml
#      explains what each of them would do to the co-tenants.
#
# Prove the result rather than assuming it:
#   pnpm db:check --db-url postgresql://...    read-only, safe on production
#   pnpm db:roundtrip --db-url postgresql://... behaviour, always rolled back
#
# WHICH OF THESE GO IN VERCEL:
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL              yes — public, ships to the browser
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY  yes — public, ships to the browser
#   SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY             yes — SERVER ONLY, never NEXT_PUBLIC_
#   CREDENTIALS_ENCRYPTION_KEY            yes — SERVER ONLY, never NEXT_PUBLIC_
#   CRON_SECRET                           yes — SERVER ONLY, and see its note
#   X_SEARCH_QUERY                        yes, IF X IS TO RUN — it is not a
#   X_MAX_POSTS_PER_RUN                   secret, but X is unavailable without
#                                         both, and the app caps the second at
#                                         its own ceiling whatever is set here
#   X_WINDOW_HOURS                        optional, a cost control
#   X_POST_FIELDS_PARAM                   only if an error message asks for it
#   PLATFORM_SEEDS_*                      only for a deployment with NO database.
#                                         With Supabase configured these are
#                                         IGNORED; the list is derived from runs
#   SUPABASE_DB_URL                       NO  — laptop tool only, see below
#   *_API_KEY and the Meta app fields     NO  — development fallbacks, see below
#   QUOTA_DIR                             NO  — laptop tool only, see below
#
# Scope them to the PRODUCTION environment. Vercel hands Preview and
# Development the same values otherwise, which means a pull-request preview
# writes to the production inventory. Deploying with NONE of them set is a
# supported, deliberate state — the app renders and says it has no database.
# README "Deploying" has the whole argument.
#
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY` is accepted as a fallback for the publishable
# key (lib/supabase/config.ts prefers PUBLISHABLE and falls back to ANON), for
# projects created before Supabase renamed it. Set one, not both. It is not
# given a line of its own here because a name on a line reads as a name to
# fill in, and the one to fill in is PUBLISHABLE.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# The direct Postgres connection string, used ONLY by verify/db.ts. Not read by
# the app. Dashboard -> Settings -> Database -> Connection string -> URI.
#
# DO NOT SET THIS ON VERCEL. It is a laptop tool's variable, and nothing the
# app serves ever reads it. It reaches the database around PostgREST, RLS and
# every grant in the schema — including the co-tenants' — so there is no
# version of "it is already there, might as well" that is worth it.
SUPABASE_DB_URL=

# SERVER ONLY, and used for exactly one thing: calling lease_api_credential(),
# the SECURITY DEFINER function that hands an ingest worker the plaintext of an
# operator's API key. That function is granted to `service_role` and to nothing
# else — not anon, not authenticated, not PUBLIC — so an admin's browser session
# cannot reach a key even though the admin is the person who pasted it in.
#
# NEVER put this in any NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable and never ship it to a browser.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credential encryption
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 32 random bytes, base64 or hex. Operator API keys are sealed with AES-256-GCM
# under this key before they are stored, and this key is deliberately held
# OUTSIDE the database — a database dump alone yields nothing.
#
# Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
#
# Trust assumption, stated plainly: anyone holding BOTH the database and this
# server's environment has the plaintext. Better than storing bare, worse than
# a KMS or Supabase Vault. See lib/credentials/secret-box.ts.
#
# CHANGING OR LOSING THIS VALUE IS UNRECOVERABLE. There is no second copy and
# no re-wrap path — that is the point of holding it outside the database. Every
# stored operator key stops decrypting at once and every operator has to paste
# theirs in again. It fails late, as ingest failing to lease a credential, not
# at deploy time. Generate it once and keep it.
CREDENTIALS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The data provider — ScrapeCreators (Instagram, TikTok, Facebook)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DECIDED by Erik on 2026-09-04: "We are going with ScrapeCreators". Bright Data
# was offered as the more RELIABLE option and declined at roughly eight times
# the price, with the tradeoff accepted knowingly. THE ARGUMENT LIVES IN ONE
# PLACE ONLY — plan amendment A5 in
# C:\LUCKY35\work-to-be-completed\l35-shorts-scraper-2026-004.md. It is not
# re-argued here and it must not be re-argued in the code.
#
# WHAT IT IS FOR: three of the five platforms. Instagram (seeded creator reels,
# with keyword and hashtag as a secondary sweep), TikTok (a trending feed by
# region plus keyword and hashtag search — the only REAL discovery in this
# product), and Facebook (page-seeded reels only; nobody sells Facebook
# discovery, this vendor included).
#
# WHAT IT IS NOT FOR. X stays on the official API — it already returns the view
# count, the duration and the video URL in one response and it is the only leg
# here with no third-party terms problem, so routing it through a scraper would
# add cost and a hop and gain nothing. YouTube is unchanged and keyless.
#
# BILLING IS PER REQUEST, NOT PER RECORD, and that is the whole reason this is
# affordable: one call returns a PAGE of results for one credit, so discarding
# everything under 500,000 views costs nothing extra. Read off
# https://scrapecreators.com on 2026-09-04: $47 for 25,000 credits (~$1.88 per
# 1,000), no subscription, and credits that do not expire — "Buy credits when
# you need them. Use them 2 days from now, or 2 years from now." PUBLISHED
# PRICES, DATED AND CITED. Not an invoice. NOTHING HAS EVER BEEN RUN AGAINST
# THIS API: there is no ScrapeCreators key on this machine and none in this
# project.
#
# THE SPEND CAP FOR THIS VENDOR COUNTS CALLS, NOT ROWS — the opposite shape to
# X_MAX_POSTS_PER_RUN, which counts Posts returned. The two legs need two
# different budgets and must not share one.
#
# TWO RISKS THE CODE HAS TO RESPECT, recorded here because they are properties
# of the vendor rather than of any one variable:
#   1. THEIR INSTAGRAM LEG BREAKS PERIODICALLY. Counted off
#      https://scrapecreators.com/status on 2026-09-04, Instagram endpoints
#      appear in more incidents than any other platform, most recently
#      /v1/instagram/user/reels on 25-26 Aug 2026 — the exact endpoint this
#      build depends on. A vendor 5xx, a 402, a 401 or a response that stopped
#      carrying play_count must each fail LOUDLY and SPECIFICALLY. None of them
#      may ever render as "no Instagram reels over 500,000 views".
#   2. FACEBOOK VIEW COUNTS ARE UNRELIABLE AND THE VENDOR SAYS SO. From
#      https://docs.scrapecreators.com/v1/facebook/post, read 2026-09-04: "For
#      some reels, view_count can be null or lower than the public Reels
#      badge." A local yt-dlp probe on 2026-09-04 returned 408 views on a reel
#      whose public badge read 9.8K. A number that may be an order of magnitude
#      low must be LABELLED wherever it is shown and a null carried as unknown
#      — an em dash per DESIGN.md, never a zero — not silently compared against
#      MIN_VIEWS.

# The ScrapeCreators API key. A DEVELOPMENT FALLBACK, exactly like the five
# below it: in production the operator pastes theirs on /admin/credentials and
# `resolveCredentialStore()` refuses to read this line when NODE_ENV=production.
#
# The name is the vendor's own. Their published agent skill
# (github.com/ScrapeCreators/agent-skills, read 2026-09-04) authenticates with
# `x-api-key: $SCRAPECREATORS_API_KEY`, so this is the name a developer coming
# from their documentation will already have exported.
#
# ONE KEY SERVES THREE PLATFORMS, which is a departure from every other name in
# this file: the other keys are per-platform because the accounts are. A single
# missing value here therefore takes out Instagram, TikTok and Facebook at
# once, and each of the three has to say so in its own words rather than three
# cards sharing one sentence.
SCRAPECREATORS_API_KEY=

# THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO `SCRAPECREATORS_BASE_URL` LINE HERE, AND IT IS WORTH
# KNOWING WHY BEFORE SOMEBODY ADDS ONE.
#
# The origin is a constant in the code — `SCRAPECREATORS_BASE` in
# lib/platform/scrapecreators.ts, mirrored as `SCRAPECREATORS_API_BASE` in
# lib/credentials/providers.ts — and the client takes a `baseUrl` option that
# overrides it. Nothing reads an environment variable for it, so a line here
# would be a name an operator could fill in, redeploy for, and watch change
# nothing. That is precisely the trap `PLATFORM_SEEDS_X` was, a few blocks up,
# and this file removed that one on 2026-09-04 rather than leaving it lying
# around.
#
# THE UNDERLYING RISK IS REAL EVEN THOUGH THE VARIABLE IS NOT. Every endpoint
# path in this build was READ FROM THE VENDOR'S DOCUMENTATION ON 2026-09-04 and
# not one has been called. A path or an origin that has moved fails at runtime
# looking EXACTLY like a bad key — a 404 and a 401 are both "it did not work" to
# whoever is holding the first paid key. Today, correcting one is a code change.
# If that turns out to be the wrong trade the first time the vendor moves
# something, the fix is to have the client read a variable AND to add the line
# here in the same commit — never one without the other.
#
# The documented default is https://api.scrapecreators.com, from the vendor's
# own published example: GET
# https://api.scrapecreators.com/v1/tiktok/profile?handle=charlidamelio with an
# `x-api-key` header (github.com/ScrapeCreators/agent-skills, read 2026-09-04).
# A DOCUMENTED DEFAULT, NOT A MEASURED ONE. Nothing here has resolved that host.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-provider API keys — DEVELOPMENT FALLBACKS ONLY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE THREE META/TIKTOK NAMES BELOW ARE NO LONGER THE ROUTE THOSE PLATFORMS RUN
# ON. As of 2026-09-04 Instagram, TikTok and Facebook read through
# ScrapeCreators (the section above). The official slots stay because what
# Meta's own API can and cannot do is a TESTED FINDING worth keeping — see plan
# amendment A4 — and a credential slot that says "Meta documents no read here"
# is worth more than a deleted one. X and YouTube are unaffected.
#
# IN PRODUCTION THIS IS NOT WHERE ANY KEY COMES FROM. Every operator supplies
# their own on /admin/credentials; keys are stored per-tenant and encrypted at
# rest, so the operator's own developer account carries their quota, their
# billing and their terms-of-service exposure. `resolveCredentialStore()`
# refuses to read any of these when NODE_ENV=production.
#
# These five exist so a developer can poke at an API on a laptop. If a run's
# logs say `environment` rather than `database`, somebody's laptop key is
# paying for it — lib/credentials/env-store.ts labels every lease that way on
# purpose.
#
# THE NAMES ARE DECLARED, NOT DERIVED. lib/credentials/providers.ts spells all
# five out in a table rather than computing `${ID}_API_KEY`, so that grepping
# for INSTAGRAM_API_KEY finds where it is read. `X_API_KEY` is generic enough
# to collide with something else on a shared machine; it is still the
# mechanical name, because the alternative is one special case in a table of
# five and a developer guessing which platform got the exception.

# YouTube Data API v3 key, from your own Google Cloud project. An UPGRADE, not
# a requirement: YouTube can be read without one. What it adds is publish
# dates, which the keyless walk carried for none of the 137 uploads in
# lib/platform/fixtures/ytdlp-uploads-137.json.
YOUTUBE_API_KEY=

# X (Twitter) Bearer Token, from console.x.com.
#
# THIS ONE COSTS MONEY PER RESULT AND A SPEND CAP MATTERS. Checked 2026-09-04
# against https://docs.x.com/x-api/getting-started/pricing: reading posts is
# "$0.005 per resource" — per post RETURNED, not per request made — with a
# ceiling of "3 million Post reads per monthly billing cycle". There are no
# subscription tiers to run out of; you buy credits and they drain. The cycle
# cap is a backstop and not a budget: reaching it costs about $15,000.
#
# Two mitigations that are properties of the API and worth knowing:
#   - "Same resource requested twice in 24 hours is only charged once", so a
#     schedule that re-reads the same accounts costs far less than the number
#     of runs suggests.
#   - /2/tweets/counts/recent is "$0.005 per request" — a flat, per-request
#     probe that tells you how much a query would return BEFORE you pay per
#     resource to return it.
#
# PUBLISHED PRICES, DATED AND CITED. Not an invoice. Nothing has been run.
#
# A TOKEN ON ITS OWN DOES NOT MAKE X RUN. It also needs X_SEARCH_QUERY and
# X_MAX_POSTS_PER_RUN, in the section above — what to search for and how much of
# it you are willing to buy. The adapter names whichever is missing rather than
# returning an empty list.
X_API_KEY=

# Instagram: FOUR VALUES, not one. lib/credentials/fields.ts is the table that
# declares them and it is the file to read before changing any of these names.
#
#   INSTAGRAM_API_KEY               the long-lived access token (the secret)
#   INSTAGRAM_APP_ID                Meta app dashboard -> App settings -> Basic
#   INSTAGRAM_APP_SECRET            same page, behind "Show"
#   INSTAGRAM_BUSINESS_ACCOUNT_ID   YOUR OWN Instagram business account's id
#
# THE FOURTH IS THE ONE PEOPLE FORGET, and it is required rather than optional
# for a mechanical reason: Business Discovery is read FROM a node, not from a
# global search endpoint —
#   GET /<IG_USER_ID>?fields=business_discovery.username(<USERNAME>)
# — and `<IG_USER_ID>` is the account doing the asking, not the creator being
# asked about. Without it there is no request to make. Find it with
# `GET /<page-id>?fields=instagram_business_account`.
#
# The app id and app secret are here because the credential check calls
# `debug_token`, whose documented shortcut for an app access token is passing
# `{app-id}|{app-secret}`. The app id is not a secret and is stored readable.
#
# READ plan amendment A4 BEFORE SPENDING WEEKS ON THIS. Verified 2026-09-04:
# Business Discovery returns a named account's media WITH view_count but cannot
# search; Hashtag Search searches but returns NO view count and NO duration and
# is capped at 30 unique hashtags per rolling 7 days behind the restricted
# "Instagram Public Content Access" feature. And there is NO duration field on
# an Instagram media object at all, on either route — so SHORT_MAX_SECONDS
# cannot be applied to official Instagram data. View counts or discovery, never
# both, and never a duration.
INSTAGRAM_API_KEY=
INSTAGRAM_APP_ID=
INSTAGRAM_APP_SECRET=
INSTAGRAM_BUSINESS_ACCOUNT_ID=

# Facebook: FOUR VALUES, the same Meta triple plus the Page.
#
#   FACEBOOK_API_KEY   the long-lived PAGE access token (the secret)
#   FACEBOOK_APP_ID    Meta app dashboard -> App settings -> Basic
#   FACEBOOK_APP_SECRET  same page, behind "Show"
#   FACEBOOK_PAGE_ID   the Page the operator administers
#
# `FACEBOOK_PAGE_ID` IS REQUIRED, AND THAT IS A PRODUCT STATEMENT RATHER THAN A
# FORM CONVENIENCE: Facebook's official API has no cross-account search at all,
# so a Facebook credential that names no Page has nothing it could read.
#
# Meta documents long-lived tokens as lasting about 60 days and warns the
# lifetime can change without notice, so both Meta providers need re-pasting on
# a cadence nobody controls. That is a support cost, and it is worth weighing
# before deciding these are worth having.
#
# EXPECT THIS TO FAIL, AND THAT IS THE POINT. Checked 2026-09-04, Meta's own
# Graph API reference documents NO read operation on either /{page-id}/videos
# or /{page-id}/video_reels — the Reading section of both reads "You can't
# perform this operation on this endpoint." There is no public-content search
# on Facebook either. The Meta Content Library (the CrowdTangle replacement) is
# academic and non-profit only and Lucky35 is explicitly ineligible; do not
# build toward it and do not route an application through anyone who is
# eligible, because the application carries an eligibility representation.
#
# The slot exists so the failure is a TESTED FINDING with a date on it rather
# than an opinion somebody re-litigates in three months.
FACEBOOK_API_KEY=
FACEBOOK_APP_ID=
FACEBOOK_APP_SECRET=
FACEBOOK_PAGE_ID=

# Threads: ONE VALUE, AND IT IS NOT A META TOKEN.
#
# Added 2026-09-08. Threads is the only Meta surface that can be asked about a
# SUBJECT rather than about accounts you already administer: its keyword search
# (graph.threads.net/v1.0/keyword_search) returns other people's public video
# posts for a topic's terms. Instagram and Facebook cannot do that at any price,
# which is why this slot exists at all.
#
# IT IS ITS OWN CREDENTIAL. The token comes from threads.net's own OAuth, has
# its own scopes and goes to its own host. A FACEBOOK_API_KEY pasted here
# authenticates nothing, and this token pasted into the Facebook or Instagram
# slot fails there — same company, different APIs.
#
# THE SCOPE IS THE WHOLE STORY, AND GETTING IT WRONG IS SILENT. The token needs
# threads_basic AND threads_keyword_search, and the second one needs App Review.
# With only the first, the search endpoint still answers 200 and still returns
# results — just the holder's OWN posts — so a topic run comes back empty and
# reads as a quiet day rather than as a misconfiguration. The Test button on
# /admin/credentials performs a real keyword search for exactly this reason.
#
# AND IT CAN NEVER MEASURE ANYTHING. Meta publishes view counts only for your
# own posts (/{threads-media-id}/insights) and publishes no duration field
# anywhere in the Threads API. So every row this platform returns has unknown
# views and unknown length, and the run reports them as unverified rather than
# counting them against the 500,000 threshold. This key buys DISCOVERY, not
# evidence — decide whether the App Review paperwork is worth it on that basis.
#
# There is deliberately no PLATFORM_SEEDS_THREADS. Threads is searched by
# keyword, never by account, exactly like X — a seed list would be a name for a
# thing this adapter has no way to read.
THREADS_API_KEY=

# TikTok: THERE IS STILL NO OFFICIAL TIKTOK KEY. All three official TikTok APIs
# — Research, Display and Commercial Content — are closed to this use, and that
# has not changed. TikTok is read keylessly through yt-dlp and, since
# 2026-09-04, through SCRAPECREATORS_API_KEY above — which is a different name,
# on purpose: it is one credential covering three platforms, not TikTok's own.
# This name exists because lib/credentials/providers.ts gives all five platforms
# a slot; a key saved here is encrypted and then sits unused, and the settings
# page says so rather than implying a reader that does not exist.
TIKTOK_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# YouTube quota
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A daily unit allowance, for the development case only. The real figure lives
# per credential on `api_credentials.daily_quota_units`, because quota is per
# Google Cloud project and one number cannot be right for two operators.
#
# THIS BOUNDS ONE OF TWO ALLOWANCES, AND NOT THE ONE THAT BINDS. Checked on
# 2026-09-04 against
# https://developers.google.com/youtube/v3/determine_quota_cost, a project gets
# a shared daily pool of units spent by playlistItems.list, videos.list and
# channels.list at 1 unit per call — which is what this variable caps — AND, in
# a bucket of its own, a hard ration of 100 search.list calls a day. Google:
# "The search.list and videos.insert methods have their own quota buckets. Each
# of these methods has a default daily limit of 100 per day."
#
# The two do not exchange. Spending less here buys no extra searches, and a
# quota increase on the pool does not raise the search ceiling. So a run that
# stays honestly inside this number can still 403 on search.list, and nothing
# you can write on this line will prevent that. Discovery is capped in CALLS;
# seeded enumeration of named channels is capped in units and, at 1 unit per 50
# videos, is effectively uncapped for this workload. That is the whole reason
# the tool walks the uploads playlist instead of searching.
#
# There is DELIBERATELY no default anywhere, and the figures named above are
# GOOGLE'S PUBLISHED CLAIM, dated and cited — not a measurement, and not a
# value to paste onto this line. A daily-quota figure that is not a measurement
# is exactly what this project refuses to hold — see
# lib/config.ts:dailyQuotaUnits() and verify/fixtures/README.md.
YOUTUBE_DAILY_QUOTA_UNITS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The scheduled run
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SERVER ONLY. A random string of at least 16 characters, generated once.
#
# WHAT READS IT: app/api/cron/run/route.ts, which is the door runOnSchedule()
# did not have until 2026-09-04. Before that, this block described a refusal
# that did not exist — lib/shorts/schedule.ts was complete and tested and
# NOTHING CALLED IT, and this file was the only place in the tree the name
# CRON_SECRET appeared at all.
#
# The route accepts GET and POST at `/api/cron/run`. GET because that is what
# Vercel Cron sends; POST because it is the honest method for a call that spends
# money and writes rows. From anything else:
#
#   curl -fsS -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET" \
#     "https://<deployment>/api/cron/run?slice=1"
#
# THE NAME IS VERCEL'S, not ours. Checked 2026-09-04 against
# https://vercel.com/docs/cron-jobs/manage-cron-jobs: setting an environment
# variable called `CRON_SECRET` makes Vercel send its value automatically as an
# `Authorization: Bearer <value>` header when it invokes a cron job, and the
# route compares the two. Renaming it would mean Vercel sends one thing and the
# route expects another, so this name is not a preference.
#
# WITHOUT IT AN UNATTENDED TRIGGER WOULD BE A PUBLIC URL, and on X a run costs
# money per Post returned. So the route treats it as a refusal, not a
# permissive default: unset, blank, or SHORTER THAN 16 CHARACTERS answers 503
# and never runs. A one-character secret is not a smaller amount of protection,
# it is a public URL with a formality in front of it. The presented value is
# compared in constant time over SHA-256 digests rather than the strings, so
# neither the content nor the LENGTH of the real secret leaks through response
# timing, and the comparison runs even when the header is missing so a
# well-formed guess and a blank request take the same path.
#
# ONE FIRE DOES A SLICE OF WORK. `?slice=N` bounds how many platforms a single
# invocation will claim and DEFAULTS TO ONE, because a run stores everything in
# a single write at the end of the pass — so a serverless invocation killed
# halfway through five platforms would lose the results of the ones that had
# already finished, including, on X, results that were paid for. One platform
# per fire makes the unit of loss one platform. A host with real time (a systemd
# timer, a GitHub runner) should ask for `?slice=5`.
#
# THE CADENCE IS NOT IN THIS FILE, AND THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO vercel.json. A
# Vercel cron must be declared there under "crons" and the route alone does not
# create one; adding it would commit the repo to Vercel's cadence rules before
# Erik has chosen a host, and `vercel.json` is strict JSON with no comment
# syntax, so the reasoning could not travel with the file. When somebody does
# choose Vercel, the whole of it is one line at the repo root:
#
#   { "crons": [{ "path": "/api/cron/run?slice=1", "schedule": "0 * * * *" }] }
#
# — plus this variable set in the PRODUCTION environment, which is what makes
# Vercel send the bearer token. On the Hobby plan a cron may run only ONCE PER
# DAY at an unpredictable minute inside the scheduled hour, and a more frequent
# expression fails the deploy outright.
#
# WHAT A SCHEDULED RUN CAN AND CANNOT DO, so nobody reads the cadence as a
# discovery rate: it refreshes the creators that have been seeded. For TikTok
# it cannot grow that list — yt-dlp's tag, sound and effect extractors are all
# marked CURRENTLY BROKEN upstream, there is no trending extractor, and all
# three official TikTok APIs are closed to this use. A list that stops growing
# stopped because nobody added to it.
#
# NOTHING IS SCHEDULED BY DEFAULT, IN TWO INDEPENDENT PLACES, AND THAT IS ABOUT
# MONEY. `platform_schedule.enabled` is FALSE for all five rows when migration
# 20260901_07_seeds_and_schedule.sql is applied and only a person can change it;
# and a trigger with no CRON_SECRET set refuses. Applying the migration and
# setting this variable still runs nothing until somebody enables a platform.
CRON_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Development-only switches
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by live code and not documented before 2026-09-04. A variable that exists
# but is not named in this file is a variable somebody finds by grep, or does
# not find at all.
#
# SHORTS_PREVIEW USED TO BE HERE and is gone as of 2026-09-04. It opened the
# /admin route group on a machine with no Supabase, so the screens could be
# looked at without standing up a database. /admin is now open to anyone who
# has the URL (Erik's call — see lib/auth/role.ts), so there is nothing left for
# it to open; `lib/shorts/preview.ts` and the flag were deleted together rather
# than left as a switch that reads as if it still controls access. If it is
# still set in a shell or on a host, nothing reads it.

# Where verify/quota.ts reads and writes its fixture directory. An override for
# running the harness against a scratch directory; unset uses the default under
# verify/fixtures/. Laptop tool only, and nothing the app serves reads it.
QUOTA_DIR=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The yt-dlp service — both, or neither
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT PROBLEM THESE SOLVE. YouTube discovery is entirely keyless yt-dlp. The
# YouTube Data API key does NOT replace it: that key hydrates rows yt-dlp has
# already found (publish dates, likes, comments) and cannot enumerate a channel
# at all. yt-dlp is a binary, and Vercel's runtime has neither the binary nor a
# way to install one, so on Vercel the YouTube adapter correctly but uselessly
# reported "`yt-dlp` is not installed or not on PATH" and returned nothing.
# TikTok's keyless path is the same story.
#
# Setting BOTH of these points the app at services/ytdlp — an HTTP wrapper
# around a real yt-dlp — instead of spawning one. Setting NEITHER spawns
# locally, which is correct on a developer machine and in CI. Setting only one
# reads as neither, on purpose: a URL with no token cannot authenticate and a
# token with no URL has nowhere to go, and a half-configured deployment that
# fell back to spawning would report the wrong reason for an empty list.
#
# THE FAILURE TO EXPECT, and it is not a bug in this code: YouTube blocks
# datacentre IPs, and it blocks ordinary ones too once they have asked a few
# times in a row. A resolve that works from a laptop starts returning "Sign in
# to confirm you're not a bot", and MEASURED HERE ON 2026-09-08 the listing walk
# kept working while every media resolve from the same machine was refused — so
# a run can file shorts it cannot then hand you a file for. The service passes
# yt-dlp's own stderr back and the app puts that line on screen. The fixes are
# cookies from a signed-in session (below) or a residential proxy (not wired).

# Base URL of the service, with no trailing /run — e.g. https://ytdlp.fly.dev
YTDLP_SERVICE_URL=

# The bearer token the service requires. The service REFUSES TO START on
# anything shorter than 16 characters: it spawns processes, and this token is
# the only thing in front of it. Generate one with
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
# and set the same value here and on the service.
YTDLP_SERVICE_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# COOKIES FROM A SIGNED-IN YOUTUBE SESSION
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# What answers "Sign in to confirm you're not a bot". Set ONE of the two — both
# together is refused with a sentence naming them, because yt-dlp takes one
# cookie source and picking for you would be guessing which.
#
# USE A THROWAWAY GOOGLE ACCOUNT. These cookies are a live session: anyone who
# reads the file is signed in as that account, and YouTube does suspend accounts
# it catches being used this way. Never the account that matters.
#
# WHERE THEY APPLY. Both are read by the LOCAL runner — the machine that spawns
# yt-dlp. On a deployment using the remote service, set YTDLP_COOKIES_FILE on
# THE SERVICE instead (see services/ytdlp/README.md): the app cannot send a
# cookie flag to it, deliberately, because a caller who could name a path could
# read files off that host.

# A browser profile on this machine: chrome, firefox, edge, brave, chromium,
# opera, safari, vivaldi, whale — optionally BROWSER:PROFILE.
#
# MEASURED ON WINDOWS, 2026-09-08: this does not work with Chrome or Edge.
# Chrome answers "Could not copy Chrome cookie database" while it is open, and
# both answer "Failed to decrypt with DPAPI" once it is closed — app-bound
# encryption, which yt-dlp cannot read. On Windows, export a file instead.
YTDLP_COOKIES_FROM_BROWSER=

# A cookies.txt in Netscape format, exported from a signed-in browser with an
# extension such as "Get cookies.txt LOCALLY". Works on every platform and is
# the only thing that works on Windows. It EXPIRES — when downloads start
# refusing again with the same sentence, export a fresh one.
YTDLP_COOKIES_FILE=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A PROXY — THE ONLY LEVER HERE THAT NEEDS NO ACCOUNT AND NO HUMAN STEP
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Cookies need somebody to sign in somewhere, once, ever: YouTube killed the
# OAuth device-code flow, so there is no way left to mint a session
# unattended. A proxy needs nobody — it is an address, set once, and every
# yt-dlp call from every host goes through it.
#
# MEASURED 2026-09-08 and this is what says the address is the problem: with
# this machine's IP gated, yt-dlp 2026.08.19 and a working BgUtils PO token
# provider on localhost:4416 were refused exactly like the old build, on every
# player client. The refusal lands before a token is even requested.
#
# IT IS CHEAPER THAN IT SOUNDS. Residential proxies bill per gigabyte, and this
# tool never downloads a video: the listing is JSON and the media step is
# --print urls, which implies --simulate. The proxy carries metadata.
#
# Whole URL, scheme included. http, https, socks4, socks4a, socks5 or socks5h.
# The password in it is scrubbed out of anything that reaches a screen or a log.
# On a deployment using the remote runner, set this ON THE SERVICE instead —
# that is where the request leaves from.
YTDLP_PROXY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THE PROXY COSTS, SO THE SCREEN CAN SAY SO
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Dollars per gigabyte, as your provider bills it — "3.50", not "$3.50/GB".
#
# NO DEFAULT, DELIBERATELY. Providers quote anywhere from $1 to $7 for the same
# product and the rate falls with volume, so any figure this repo picked would
# be a price on somebody else's invoice. Left unset, every cost on the page is
# stated in MEGABYTES and the money is simply absent — which is honest, and
# still tells an operator what a press will spend.
#
# WHAT IT PRICES. Reading is metadata and effectively free; the FILES are the
# spend. Measured 2026-09-08 against the live service, two 720p H.264 shorts:
# 19,071,177 and 19,174,282 bytes. So roughly 19MB a download, and a 3GB plan is
# about 160 of them. Set this and the page quotes both.
YTDLP_PROXY_USD_PER_GB=
